WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.486 --> 00:00:22.427
Marius had entered Monsieur Gillenormand's house with little hope. He exited it in deep despair.

2
00:00:22.428 --> 00:00:27.088
He began to walk the streets of Paris, not knowing what else to do.

3
00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:31.222
At two o'clock in the morning, he returned to Courfeyrac's apartment.

4
00:00:31.223 --> 00:00:37.143
Marius threw himself on his mattress fully dressed; it was dawn before he fell asleep.

5
00:00:37.144 --> 00:00:44.113
When Marius woke up, Courfeyrac was there. So were two other friends, Enjolras and Combeferre.

6
00:00:44.114 --> 00:00:46.802
The three of them were getting ready to go out.

7
00:00:46.803 --> 00:00:49.818
"Are you coming with us?" asked Courfeyrac.

8
00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:56.069
Marius shook his head. Sometime after they left, he put Javert's pistol into his pocket.

9
00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:02.223
After a while he went out and roamed the streets again, dark thoughts flooding his mind.

10
00:01:02.224 --> 00:01:09.286
At nine o'clock he arrived at the house on the Rue Plumet. It had been 48 hours since he had last seen Cosette.

11
00:01:09.287 --> 00:01:15.453
Now he was going to see her again, and all other thoughts faded away.

12
00:01:15.454 --> 00:01:22.857
He pushed aside the bar on the gate and entered the garden. Cosette was not in the spot where she usually waited for him.

13
00:01:22.858 --> 00:01:26.945
"She must be waiting for me on the bench," he said to himself.

14
00:01:26.946 --> 00:01:30.266
But she was not there. He looked up at the house.

15
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:35.112
All the shutters were closed. He walked around the garden and returned to the house.

16
00:01:35.113 --> 00:01:41.270
Mad with love, he pounded on the door. He did not care if her father answered the door.

17
00:01:41.271 --> 00:01:45.770
"Cosette! Cosette!" cried Marius in a loud voice.

18
00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:51.279
There was no response. Nobody was in the garden, nobody in the house.

19
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.756
Marius fixed his eyes on that house as dark and silent as a tomb.

20
00:01:55.757 --> 00:01:59.030
Feeling hopeless, he sat on the steps.

21
00:01:59.031 --> 00:02:04.835
"Since Cosette has gone," he said to himself, "there's nothing for me to do but die."

22
00:02:04.836 --> 00:02:09.320
Suddenly he heard a voice coming from the street beyond the garden gate.

23
00:02:09.321 --> 00:02:11.344
"Monsieur Marius!"

24
00:02:11.345 --> 00:02:14.320
He arose. "Yes?"

25
00:02:14.321 --> 00:02:21.407
"Monsieur Marius, your friends are expecting you at the barricade on the Rue de la Chanvrerie."

26
00:02:21.408 --> 00:02:26.041
The voice sounded like Eponine's. Marius rushed to the gate and looked out.

27
00:02:26.042 --> 00:02:30.550
He saw someone who looked like a young man rapidly disappearing down the street.

28
00:02:30.551 --> 00:02:32.668
What was this barricade?

29
00:02:32.669 --> 00:02:40.883
While Marius had been in despair, Courfeyrac and the others had joined what would later be called the Paris Uprising of 1832.

30
00:02:40.884 --> 00:02:48.875
A large group of students and workers decided to rebel against the monarchy, and they took their fight into the streets of Paris.

31
00:02:48.876 --> 00:02:56.050
Outside the Corinth Tavern, fifty men were building a barricade to block the Rue de la Chanvrerie.

32
00:02:56.051 --> 00:03:00.184
Iron bars were ripped from the grates in front of the tavern.

33
00:03:00.185 --> 00:03:07.034
Twenty yards of pavement were torn up. Piles of stones and barrels were also added.

34
00:03:07.035 --> 00:03:11.589
An omnibus was even tipped over and incorporated into the barricade.

35
00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:17.801
Despite all these preparations, the barricade did not exceed six or seven feet in height.

36
00:03:17.802 --> 00:03:21.343
Similar barricades were going up throughout Paris.

37
00:03:21.344 --> 00:03:28.430
When the barricade was completed, a table was dragged from the tavern, and Courfeyrac distributed ammunition.

38
00:03:28.431 --> 00:03:36.414
There was not much ammunition; each man received only thirty cartridges. A powder keg sat nearby.

39
00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:43.790
The fifty men in the Chanvrerie barricade loaded their guns and waited as darkness fell over Paris.

40
00:03:43.791 --> 00:03:48.319
Nothing happened. The government forces were taking their time.

41
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:54.390
Among the men in the barricade was the street urchin Gavroche, who was also Eponine's brother.

42
00:03:54.391 --> 00:04:03.565
While the rebels waited for the attack, Gavroche looked around. Suddenly he recognized someone, a new recruit to the rebellion.

43
00:04:03.566 --> 00:04:08.035
Gavroche ran to Enjolras, one of the leaders in the barricade.

44
00:04:08.036 --> 00:04:12.636
"See that fellow over there?" Gavroche indicated a tall man.

45
00:04:12.637 --> 00:04:19.390
"I think he's a spy! He's really a policeman; two weeks ago he tried to arrest me."

46
00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:24.891
Enjolras approached the man. "What are you doing here? What's your name?"

47
00:04:24.892 --> 00:04:28.874
"I'm an officer of the government and my name is Javert."

48
00:04:28.875 --> 00:04:35.620
Enjolras gave a signal and four men pounced on Javert. They threw him to the ground and searched him.

49
00:04:35.621 --> 00:04:40.366
They found a piece of paper instructing him to look for the rebels' hideouts.

50
00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:47.271
"You are a spy, and you'll be shot ten minutes before the barricade is seized," said Enjolras.

51
00:04:47.272 --> 00:04:51.358
"Why don't you shoot me now?" asked Javert.

52
00:04:51.359 --> 00:04:54.176
"We don't want to waste the ammunition."

53
00:04:54.177 --> 00:05:01.551
By this time Marius was heading for the barricade. He knew it would be a dangerous place, but he no longer cared.

54
00:05:01.552 --> 00:05:04.938
It was clear that Cosette no longer loved him.

55
00:05:04.939 --> 00:05:11.587
She had left without sending him a single word. There was no excuse for this because she knew his address.

56
00:05:11.588 --> 00:05:15.089
He could not abandon his friends, who clearly needed him.

57
00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:21.414
They were perhaps a handful of men against an entire army that was defending the monarchy.

58
00:05:21.415 --> 00:05:29.327
Forty-three rebels crouched behind the barricade. The other seven were stationed in the two upper stories of the Corinth Tavern.

59
00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:38.137
Suddenly there was the sound of marching footsteps. A loud explosion burst over the barricade. The attack had begun!

60
00:05:38.138 --> 00:05:44.312
Two National Guards climbed over the barricade. One knocked Courfeyrac to the ground.

61
00:05:44.313 --> 00:05:47.240
The other threatened Gavroche with his bayonet.

62
00:05:47.241 --> 00:05:55.050
Two shots rang out, and the guards fell over. Marius had just entered the barricade and fired Javert's pistol.

63
00:05:55.051 --> 00:06:03.471
More soldiers entered the barricade, but Marius was out of ammunition. As he frantically looked around, a guard aimed at him.

64
00:06:03.472 --> 00:06:08.691
Suddenly a young workingman in velvet trousers reached out in front of Marius.

65
00:06:08.692 --> 00:06:12.955
When the gun fired, it was the workingman who fell to the ground.

66
00:06:12.956 --> 00:06:20.709
Marius looked around again and spotted the keg of gunpowder. "Clear out or I'll blow up the barricade!" he shouted.

67
00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:24.534
Everyone turned to look at him as he picked up a lit torch.

68
00:06:24.535 --> 00:06:29.534
"If you blow up the barricade, you'll blow up yourself too," said a soldier.

69
00:06:29.535 --> 00:06:33.860
"That's right." Marius waved the torch near the keg.

70
00:06:33.861 --> 00:06:41.757
The soldiers fled over the top of the barricade as fast as they could."Who's in charge here?" Marius asked the rebels.

71
00:06:41.758 --> 00:06:49.352
"You are!" said Enjolras, patting him on the back. Other men did too. Marius was a hero.

72
00:06:49.353 --> 00:06:52.244
"Monsieur Marius!" cried a voice.

73
00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:56.408
Marius recognized Eponine's voice, but he didn't see her anywhere.

74
00:06:56.409 --> 00:06:59.503
"At your feet," said Eponine.

75
00:06:59.504 --> 00:07:05.749
Marius bent over. The torchlight revealed the face of the young man in the velvet trousers.

76
00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.625
"Eponine! What are you doing here?"

77
00:07:08.626 --> 00:07:10.281
"I'm dying."

78
00:07:10.282 --> 00:07:12.233
"I'll carry you into the tavern."

79
00:07:12.234 --> 00:07:23.241
"No . . ." She struggled to talk. "It's too late. I used my hand to stop the bullet intended for you."

80
00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:27.392
"No one dies from a wounded hand," protested Marius.

81
00:07:27.393 --> 00:07:32.884
"The bullet entered my chest," she said weakly.

82
00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:42.463
"I didn't want you to go to that garden, and yet I showed you where she lived. But it's all right now.

83
00:07:42.464 --> 00:07:49.042
Nobody will get out alive from this barricade—not even my brother.

84
00:07:49.043 --> 00:07:56.455
I sent you here so we could die together because I've always been in love with you."

85
00:07:56.456 --> 00:07:59.463
Marius gazed at her with compassion.

86
00:07:59.464 --> 00:08:05.693
"I have a letter for you." Eponine held out her hand.

87
00:08:05.694 --> 00:08:12.046
"I was supposed to deliver it yesterday, but I didn't want it to reach you.

88
00:08:12.047 --> 00:08:20.440
Now that I know we'll be reunited after death, I don't want you to be angry with me."

89
00:08:20.441 --> 00:08:25.837
Eponine died, and Marius took the letter from her hand.
